a parade to mark Lebanon's independence  day as the country lives through  unprecedented times this was a display  of civil society united against the  political system many now want to change  the protests which began on October the  17th of force Prime Minister saddle had  a view to resign but that hasn't been  enough for demonstrators they want to  see the back of the entire political  elite the entire political system the  banking system the social system it's  everyone here is demonstrating against  that and for the first time in the  history of Lebanon it is decentralized  as a revolution and it is non-sectarian  based on Lebanon sectarian Constitution  consensus must be reached between all  sides to form a government that needs to  be approved by Parliament on the one  side Herrera and his Sony bloc and their  allies refused to accept the government  that isn't formed totally of technocrats  experts and professionals rather than  politicians President Michelle own and  his Hezbollah and I say that's a coup  against democracy since they have a  majority in parliament at a military  parade earlier on Friday an admission by  both sides that a solution had to be  found I think it's pretty deep I think  it's very deep we have to eventually sit  on some sort of a table so that we can  try to reach a convergence and you know  the different views that are being  offered by the two sides so if we don't  do that pretty soon I think we will have  a problem in my opinion we have to find  a combination of political support for  any government that we formed because of  the vote of confidence that is needed at  the Parliament at the same time those  blocks that are willing to name a  government and name ministers they  should understand that we cannot do  things business as usual things are  different now  we heard the voice of the people we  respected we should respect it at the  core of the unrest is the dire economic  situation here the  of public services the constant  electricity cuts and the endemic and  institutionalized corruption that sucks  away at Lebanon's resources for that  protesters blame leaders on all sides of  the political divide independence day  there is an acute awareness of just how  dependent this country's future is on  outside forces now the protest movements  has been an attempt at achieving and  realizing some sort of  self-determination but there is a fear  amongst many the regional powers be in  Saudi Arabia or Iran won't allow them to  succeed from Allah al al Jazeera Beirut  [Music]
 

